# Mobile tab bar layout

The bottom tab bar (and tablet left rail) is a stored, device-local order. **More** is always last.
Hidden content tabs stay registered in the navigator and appear as rows on More.

Factory visible order is **Home, Browse, Search, My Library** (`DEFAULT_VISIBLE_TABS`).
Notifications is not a content tab. It is not in `CONTENT_TAB_IDS`, not a More overflow row,
and not a row on Settings > Tab Bar. The Home bell is the in-app control that opens the inbox.
A link or push whose path is `notifications` opens root route `NotificationsInbox`. The unread
count is that bell's badge, not a tab badge.

English bottom-tab labels follow **ui-copy-casing** (`nav.tab.my_library` → `My Library`), the
same title case as other chrome. Stack body copy stays sentence case.

## Do

- Read `tabs.visible` under the splash (`TabLayoutProvider`) before mounting `TabScaffold`.
- Keep `Tab.Screen` declaration order stable. Render the stored order through `OrderedTabBar`.
- Persist with `writeVisibleTabs` / `prefs/tabLayout.ts` (AsyncStorage). Do not sync this to the
  server.
- Cap visible content tabs at `MAX_VISIBLE_CONTENT_TABS` (4). Adding a fifth replaces the last slot.
- List overflow tabs on More (`more-nav-<slug>`). Opening a row pushes that tab's stack on the
  More stack (`MoreOverflowHome`, `MoreOverflowSearch`, `MoreOverflowLibrary`,
  `MoreOverflowBrowse`, `MoreOverflowRecommendations`). More stays selected and Back returns to
  More.

## Do not

- Unmount a tab to hide it (breaks `navigate('Search')` and deep links).
- Open an overflow row with `tabNavigation.navigate(tabId)`. That focuses a tab whose button is
  hidden, highlights Home, and shows no Back control.
- Paint the default bar and then swap after AsyncStorage (FOUC).
- Reorder by changing `Tab.Screen` children order (remounts stacks).
- Use the default tab press highlight (ripple / opacity). Visible tabs use `QuietTabBarButton`
  (`pressOpacity={1}`, transparent ripple). That is an exception to `Button`'s press dim.

